# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: epictetus-4.logic.r2 has nothing from it; epictetus-4.logic.r2.2 and epictetus-4.logic.r2.3 have "in_power → ¬reason_anxious"; they differ when only "the thing one is anxious about (assent) is in one's own power" and "there is reason to be anxious" are true, which the first allows
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps epictetus-4.logic.r2.2 and epictetus-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the thing one is anxious about (assent) is in one's own power" and "there is reason to be anxious" are true. Is S5, "If a man be of such a good disposition as to be anxious about these things I will remind him of this: Why are you anxious?", true then?
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 2 ways: epictetus-4.logic.r2 has "in_power → ¬reason_anxious"; epictetus-4.logic.r2.2 and epictetus-4.logic.r2.3 have "in_power"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps epictetus-4.logic.r2.2 and epictetus-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the thing one is anxious about (assent) is in one's own power" and "there is reason to be anxious" are false. Is S6, "The thing is in your own power, be assured; do not be precipitate in assenting before you apply the natural rule.", true then?
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/unfaithful: epictetus-4.logic.r2 P3 (S6): the sentence has no word of "there is reason to be anxious": is it the sentence P3 came from?
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/unfaithful: epictetus-4.logic.r2 C (S10): the sentence has no word of "one desires nothing in the power of others and tries to avoid nothing not in one's power": is it the sentence C came from?
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/unfaithful: epictetus-4.logic.r2.2 C (S10): the sentence has no word of "one desires nothing in the power of others and tries to avoid nothing not in one's power": is it the sentence C came from?
epictetus-4.md:1: argue/unfaithful: epictetus-4.logic.r2.3 C (S10): the sentence has no word of "one desires nothing in the power of others and tries to avoid nothing not in one's power": is it the sentence C came from?
